


Jonah said, “It didn’t follow me.”




Maren kept one hand near his elbow. “We can talk downstairs.”




“It wanted me gone.”




“You left eight hours ago.”




“That’s not what I mean.”




“I know.”




They descended slowly. At the blood smear on the first-floor stud, Jonah paused.




“Did I do that?”




“Probably.”




“I don’t remember.”




“We’ll photograph it in daylight if we need to.”




He looked at her. “You said you weren’t filming.”




“I’m not.”




“Then why would we need it?”




She could not explain why they might need the photograph without admitting that she was already considering how to use it.




They crossed the lot. She pulled the fence panel back into place as far as she could, then walked Jonah to the car. He sat in the passenger seat while she took the first-aid kit from the equipment case. Under the dome light, she rinsed his hand with bottled water, checked the cuts again, and wrapped the knuckles in gauze.




Jonah watched her work.




“The page said I didn’t want to go back,” he said.




“I saw it.”




“I don’t.”




Maren taped the bandage. “Good.”




“No.” He flexed his fingers and winced. “That’s worse.”




“You’re exhausted.”




“What if I wake up tomorrow and decide it was nothing?”




“That could happen.”




“What if I decide it was everything?”




“Then make that decision after you sleep.”




He looked past her toward the unfinished house. No movement showed in the windows. The loose plastic struck the frame again.




Maren closed the passenger door.




Before she could walk around the hood, Jonah lowered the window.




“We have to go back,” he said.




She stopped beside the front tire.




“No.”




“One night. Everything running from the start.”




“Jonah.”




“You heard the sound too.”




“At the preserve.”




“And you saw what happened after.”




“I saw you fall apart after two days without sleep.”




“Then record it properly.” His voice remained low. He did not look toward the camera case. “If nothing happens, that’s on me. But if something does, I won’t say I told you so.”




Maren looked at the bandage around his hand, the notebook closed on his lap, and the dark zippered pocket that held the card.




Across the street, the house wrap struck the frame once more.




Jonah waited.




Maren had already brought the camera into the unfinished house. She had recorded enough afterward to make his distress look like proof.




Now he was asking her to come back and see for herself.




Scene 2 — Terms of Return




On the monitor, forty-eight seconds separated Jonah belted into the passenger seat from the camera finding him outside.




Maren watched the interval again without sound. Jonah sat in the passenger seat with his belt fastened, his face turned toward the windshield. She looked down to check the audio level. The image shook when she shifted the camera on her lap. For eleven seconds, only his shoulder remained at the edge of the frame. Then the passenger door opened. His belt was no longer across his chest. He stepped out before the camera found him again.




There was no visible struggle. No second person crossed the headlights. In the frame, no one crossed the lit stretch between the open door and the unfinished house except Jonah.




She stopped playback before the search footage began.




The editing room occupied the back of their rented office. One wall held metal shelves loaded with cases, coiled cables, plastic bins, and battery chargers. The other held the desk, two monitors, and a narrow rack of drives beneath the tabletop. Afternoon light entered through a high window. The air conditioner clicked on, stirring the paper beside Maren’s keyboard.




At the top of the page she had typed RETURN CONDITIONS.




Below it, seven numbered lines filled most of the sheet. After-hours permission. Marked routes only. Position checks every fifteen minutes. No solo sessions. No unpublished footage without renewed consent. Entry and exit calls to Lena. All clocks synchronized before they left the office.




The eighth line read: NO EMITTER.




Maren set the playhead at the moment she entered the unfinished house. She turned the sound back on.




Her recorded breathing filled the left channel. Wind scraped the right. The camera moved through studs and exposed subfloor until its light reached Jonah. He raised his head, saw the lens, and said, “Don’t.”




Maren paused.




A cut could remove everything before that word. Viewers would see the empty car, the boot prints, the blood on the stud, Jonah in the narrow frame, and the repeated writing before they heard about exhaustion or the unrecorded sound. They could call the missing interval an attack and mistake his refusal for fear that something had followed him rather than fear of being filmed.




She dragged the clip into a new timeline and made the cuts.




The sequence lasted fifty-three seconds.




Maren watched it once. Then she deleted the timeline.




The office door opened behind her. Jonah entered carrying two paper cups and a folded clinic sheet. Fresh gauze covered the heel of his right hand. Two narrow strips held the cut below his knuckles closed. He had shaved. The skin beneath his eyes was dark.




“You’re using the card,” he said.




Maren paused playback.




“You said no one else would see it.”




“No one else has.”




He set one cup beside her keyboard. Coffee, no lid, steam rising from the dark surface. He placed the clinic sheet on top of the return conditions.




“No fracture,” he said. “No tendon damage. They cleaned it, closed it, and told me to sleep.”




“How much did you sleep?”




“Last night?”




“Since the house.”




He looked toward the equipment shelves. “Enough.”




Maren moved the clinic sheet aside and turned the return conditions toward him. “The camp is canceled.”




He did not sit. “We already discussed that.”




“We discussed going back. We did not agree to sleep there.”




“The sound happened after dark.”




“So we stay after dark.”




“It happened near two.”




“We don’t know when it happened. We know when you started talking about it in the car.”




His injured hand closed around the back of the empty chair. The movement tightened the gauze. He released it.




“What did you cut?” he asked.




“Nothing.”




“You made a timeline.”




“To see what the footage could do.”




“And?”




Maren opened the original clip. She moved the playhead to the frame where he looked at the lens. “It can make you look possessed in under a minute.”




He pulled the chair out and sat. “That isn’t what I said happened.”




“You said the preserve changed what you wanted. You said you lost time. You wrote three pages you don’t remember writing.”




“I also said I was exhausted.”




“You said that after I asked.”




“Because you asked.”




She rebuilt the cut sequence and played it without warning. The empty car appeared, then the blood, then Jonah in the studs. His recorded voice said, Don’t. The notebook filled the screen.




Maren stopped it before the repeated sentence could be read.




Jonah looked at the black monitor after the image vanished. “You left out the drive.”




“That is the point.”




“You left out everything I said before.”
